Upgrading
*********


Upgrading to Version 4.0
========================

Do you only define users in `security: users:` or the ACL in the configuration, and implement none of the library's interfaces? Then you most likely don't need to change anything in your code. **Users stay logged in:** identities that version 3.x stored in sessions are read by version 4.0.

The minimum required PHP version is 8.4, and the library needs `nette/utils` 4.1. If you use Nette Security with Nette DI or Nette HTTP, it needs `nette/di` 3.2 and `nette/http` 3.4 or newer.

.[tip]
A part of the work can be done for you by [DressCode |tools:coding-standard#Newer PHP and Nette] with the `dresscode/upgrade-nette` package: it rewrites renamed classes and constants and reports what it cannot rewrite. See [Upgrading Nette |https://dresscode.run/en/upgrading-nette].

The biggest change concerns authorization: access is now decided by [voters |authorization] instead of an authorizator that `User` asked about each role of the user. You call `$user->isAllowed()` the same way as before, and the [ACL |acl] from the configuration keeps working, because `Nette\Security\Permission` is one of the voters.


Authorization
-------------

The ACL votes like other voters, see [ACL as a Voter |acl#ACL as a Voter]. An explicit `deny()` rule forbids access even if another voter allows it. For a role or a resource the ACL doesn't know, the ACL abstains, so `$user->isAllowed()` returns `false` (unless another voter allows access) instead of throwing an exception. A typo in a resource name is therefore no longer revealed by an exception. The method `Permission::isAllowed()` is final; if your subclass of `Permission` overrides it, write a voter instead.

If your ACL comes from the configuration, `$user->getAuthorizator()` no longer returns it but throws an exception, and `getAuthorizatorIfExists()` returns `null`, because the ACL is now a voter and not an authorizator. Wherever you need the ACL itself, have the `Nette\Security\Permission` service passed to you.

Your own authorizator implementing `Nette\Security\Authorizator` still works, but it is deprecated: if it is a service, Nette reports it as deprecated and names the service. The methods `User::setAuthorizator()`, `getAuthorizator()` and `getAuthorizatorIfExists()` are deprecated as well. Rewrite it as a voter. The voter gets all the roles of the user at once, so instead of answering for one role it checks them with `isInRole()`:

```php
// before
class MyAuthorizator implements Nette\Security\Authorizator
{
	public function isAllowed(?string $role, ?string $resource, ?string $privilege): bool
	{
		return $role === 'admin' || ($role === 'editor' && $resource === 'article');
	}
}

// after
use Nette\Security\Principal;
use Nette\Security\Vote;

class MyVoter implements Nette\Security\Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		return Vote::allowIf(
			$principal->isInRole('admin')
			|| ($principal->isInRole('editor') && $resource === 'article'),
		);
	}
}
```

Are your rules naturally keyed by role? Then the voter can go through the roles one by one and keep the shape of the original method:

```php
public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
{
	return Vote::allowIf(array_any(
		$principal->getRoles(),
		fn(string $role) => $role === 'admin' || ($role === 'editor' && $resource === 'article'),
	));
}
```

Where the authorizator returned `false`, the voter abstains, so that another voter may still allow access; see [How Voters Reach a Decision |authorization#How Voters Reach a Decision].

Every voter declares the method exactly as the `Voter` interface has it, `vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote`; a narrower type such as `?string $privilege` ends with a fatal error. The privilege reaches the voter exactly as it was passed to `isAllowed()`, which besides a name now also accepts a case of an enum, see [Enums Instead of Strings |authorization#Enums Instead of Strings].

If you create the `User` object yourself, its third parameter is now a list of voters. Since `Permission` is a voter, `new User($storage, $authenticator, $acl)` keeps working. You cannot pass your own authorizator there, though; until you rewrite it as a voter, set it with the deprecated method `setAuthorizator()`.

Roles are just names. The `Nette\Security\Role` interface is deprecated, an identity whose `getRoles()` returns role objects throws an exception, and `Permission::isAllowed()` accepts the role only as a string. Assertions receive names, and `getQueriedRole()` and `getQueriedResource()` return names, too. The `Nette\Security\Resource` interface stays: it tells the ACL which of its resources an object is.

An assertion that compared the user with an entity, typically to check who owns it, is rewritten as a voter. Ownership is checked by the `hasId()` method of the [principal |authorization#Principal: Who the Decision Is About], which is never true for a user who is not logged in:

```php
// before
$acl->allow('registered', 'article', 'edit', function (Permission $acl): bool {
	$role = $acl->getQueriedRole(); // Registered object holding the user ID
	$resource = $acl->getQueriedResource(); // Article object
	return $role->id === $resource->authorId;
});

$acl->isAllowed(new Registered($user->getId()), $article, 'edit');

// after
use Nette\Security\Principal;
use Nette\Security\Vote;

class ArticleVoter implements Nette\Security\Voter
{
	public function vote(Principal $principal, mixed $resource, string|\UnitEnum|null $privilege): Vote
	{
		return Vote::allowIf(
			$resource instanceof Article
			&& $privilege === 'edit'
			&& $principal->isInRole('registered')
			&& $principal->hasId($resource->authorId),
		);
	}
}

$user->isAllowed($article, 'edit');
```

With the session storage, this also stops a logged-out user whose identity was retained. With the [cookie storage |authentication#Storage for Logged-in User], the identity never survives a logout, so there replacing `$user->getId() === $id` with `hasId($id)` changes nothing about who gets access.

The method `User::getRoles()` is final and returns the same roles as the principal. The method `User::isAllowed()` is final as well: a subclass of `User` that overrides it ends with a fatal error, so move its rules into a voter. The property `User::$authenticatedRole` is deprecated because it is never used.

Did you use `$user->isLoggedIn()` as a permission, and now want to ask through `isAllowed()`? A voter that allows everything to any logged-in user takes a single line:

```php
return Vote::allowIf($principal->isLoggedIn());
```

Don't add a role to the users in `security: users:` instead: identities already stored in sessions keep their old roles until the user logs in again.


Authentication and Identity
---------------------------

The `Nette\Security\IAuthenticator` interface has been removed; implement `Nette\Security\Authenticator`, whose method `authenticate()` takes the username and the password. The legacy service names `user`, `nette.userStorage`, `nette.authenticator` and `nette.authorizator` have been removed as well; use `security.user`, `security.userStorage`, `security.authenticator` and `security.authorizator`, or autowiring.

If you implement `Nette\Security\IIdentity` yourself, declare the return type `string|int` of `getId()` and add the method `getData()`, which returns the user data as an array. The class `Nette\Security\Identity` is now just an alias of `SimpleIdentity`. The method `IdentityHandler::getGuestIdentity()` is a regular part of the interface, so every handler must have it; return `null` if you don't use a [guest identity |authentication#Guest Identity].

Reading a key of `SimpleIdentity` that is not among its data throws `Nette\MemberAccessException`; previously it returned `null`. This also concerns adding to an array that doesn't exist yet, such as `$identity->cart[] = $item`, so create the key first: `$identity->cart = []`.

It can catch you where you don't expect it: an identity restored from the session may have been created by an older version of your own application, before you added the key. Read keys added later with `??`, such as `$identity->theme ?? 'light'`.

`SimpleIdentity` stores its ID, roles and data together with the public and protected properties of a subclass. If your subclass has private properties that should survive in the session, extend `__serialize()` and `__unserialize()`. Identities saved in sessions by version 3.x are read without problems.

With the [cookie storage |authentication#Storage for Logged-in User], the value of the cookie is only a token, which `User` never takes as the identity of the user. The authenticator must implement `IdentityHandler` and exchange the token for the identity in `wakeupIdentity()`, otherwise `User` throws `Nette\InvalidStateException`. This has been the case since version 3.2.8.

Keeping the identity after logout is on by default, so code outside voters that decides by it what the user may do must ask `$user->getPrincipal()`, and where you don't use the retained identity, switch it off with `persistIdentity: false` (see [Identity |authentication#Identity]).

The magic properties `$user->id`, `$user->roles`, `$user->logoutReason`, `$user->authenticator` and `$user->authorizator` are deprecated; call the methods `getId()`, `getRoles()`, `getLogoutReason()` and so on. The properties `$user->identity` and `$user->loggedIn` stay. The constants `User::MANUAL` and `User::INACTIVITY` have been removed; use `User::LogoutManual` and `User::LogoutInactivity`. Deprecated constants and methods now trigger `E_USER_DEPRECATED` when used.


Upgrading to Version 3.1
========================

- the `Nette\Security\Authenticator` interface differs from `IAuthenticator` in the signature of the `authenticate(string $user, string $password)` method
- the `Nette\Security\UserStorage` interface differs from `Nette\Security\IUserStorage`
- the class `Nette\Security\Identity` was renamed to `Nette\Security\SimpleIdentity` so that in the distant future the interface `Nette\Security\IIdentity` can be renamed to `Identity`
- the interface `Nette\Security\UserStorage`, which decides where the information about the logged-in user is kept, is brand new and differs from the original `Nette\Security\IUserStorage`; it is what `User::getStorage()` returns. Implementations storing the data in a session or in a cookie are available.
- an authenticator can implement the interface `Nette\Security\IdentityHandler` and thus influence what happens to the identity when it is saved to and restored from the storage, for example to refresh the roles

How to use `IdentityHandler` and how to build a website with logging in that needs no sessions at all is shown in the article [News in Nette Security 3.1 |https://blog.nette.org/en/news-in-nette-security-3-1].


Upgrading to Version 3.0
========================

- the `Nette\Security\Passwords` class is now used as an object, i.e. the methods are no longer static

